# ===========================================================================
# STAGE #2B - T(E) by transfer matrix, and an absorber that is designed
# ===========================================================================
#
# Resonance peaks a wavepacket can never resolve, and an absorbing boundary computed instead of tuned.
#
# This notebook is COMPLETE IN ITSELF. It does not depend on, and does not
# refer you to, any other notebook. Everything needed to reproduce, run,
# view and understand this stage is written out below.
#
# WHAT THIS STAGE WAS ABOUT
#
# Three gaps had been recorded. One turned out to be already closed, and
# saying so was part of the work.
#
# GAP 1 - RESONANCES NEED T(E), NOT WAVEPACKETS.
#
# A double barrier has quasi-bound states: at particular energies a wave
# tunnels through almost perfectly, and between them hardly at all. An
# earlier round tried to see those peaks by firing wavepackets and reading
# off what came through. It found a smooth monotone rise and NO peak, tried
# a four-times narrower packet in k, still found none, and recorded the
# negative result rather than tuning until something appeared.
#
# The diagnosis was right: a packet is a SUPERPOSITION of energies, so what
# it reports is T(E) AVERAGED over its own momentum spread. Narrowing the
# packet in k means widening it in x, which needs a longer domain and a
# longer run - and a resonance can always be made narrower than whatever
# packet you can afford.
#
# A transfer matrix removes the packet from the question. It solves the
# time-INDEPENDENT equation at ONE energy. The method carries (psi, psi')
# across each cell, and because both are continuous there are no interface
# matrices - the cell matrices simply multiply:
#
#     [ psi  ]     [   cos(k d)     sin(k d)/k ] [ psi  ]
#     [ psi' ]  =  [ -k sin(k d)    cos(k d)   ] [ psi' ]
#
# It is EXACT when the potential is piecewise constant on the cells, and
# second order otherwise.
#
# GAP 2 - THE ABSORBER WAS HAND-TUNED.
#
# An absorbing boundary lets a packet leave without bouncing off the wall.
# It was tuned by firing Gaussians for 5000 steps and looking. But an
# absorber is a POTENTIAL, so what it does to a wave of wavenumber k is a
# fixed-energy scattering problem - which the transfer matrix solves
# exactly, in 0.2 ms, with no packet at all.
#
# That also SEPARATES the two failure modes the packet sweep could only see
# the sum of: R is reflection off a too-strong absorber, T is leakage
# through a too-weak one. The practical conclusion follows immediately and
# could not have come from packets: over a band of wavenumbers the worst
# case is set by the LONGEST wavelength, and the cure for that is WIDTH,
# not strength.
#
# GAP 3 - '2-D NEEDS ADI' WAS STALE.
#
# The two-dimensional solver had been ADI since an earlier stage: Strang-
# composed Cayley steps, exactly unitary at any dt, O(nx*ny) per step.
# Nothing to implement. Checking cost one grep and saved building something
# that already existed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO RUN THIS NOTEBOOK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything below is posim notebook source. Lines beginning with '#' are
# comments and are ignored by the interpreter; every other line is a command
# that will be executed in order.
#
# From the repository root (the directory holding Cargo.toml), run ONE of:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook StageNbks/Stage_2B.posim
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --script   StageNbks/Stage_2B.posim
#
#   --notebook  prints an In[n]:= / Out[n]= transcript, like a Mathematica
#               notebook. This is the mode to use when reading along.
#   --script    prints the same transcript; the two differ only in how the
#               prompt lines are laid out. Either is reproducible.
#
# To work through it by hand instead, start the interactive interpreter:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release
#
# and paste the command lines one at a time. Type HELP for the full command
# language, and %quit to leave.
#
# First run will compile the workspace (a few minutes). Later runs are
# immediate. If 'cargo' is not found, install Rust from https://rustup.rs .
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCHING THE BROWSER GUI TO INVESTIGATE THIS STAGE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# posim ships a live 3-D scene window that is served over HTTP and viewed in
# an ordinary web browser. There is no separate application to install.
#
# COMPLETE PROCEDURE, from nothing to a moving picture:
#
#   1. Open a terminal at the repository root and start posim interactively:
#
#          cargo run -p posim --release
#
#      You will get the banner and an 'In[1]:=' prompt.
#
#   2. Build a scene. You must create bodies BEFORE opening the window,
#      because the window shows the entities that exist when it is created.
#      (The command lines further down this notebook do exactly that.)
#
#   3. Open the window:
#
#          scene create
#
#      posim replies with, for example:
#
#          scene window created: http://127.0.0.1:41234/
#          (opened in your browser; if no window appeared, open that address
#           yourself)
#          showing 4 entities; SCENE START begins the evolution
#
#      The port is chosen freely each run. To pin it, pass one:
#
#          scene create 8080
#
#      then the address is always http://127.0.0.1:8080/ .
#
#   4. If no browser opened by itself, copy the printed address into any
#      browser (Firefox, Chrome, Safari, Edge - it is plain HTML, CSS and
#      JavaScript over a websocket; nothing else is required).
#
#   5. Drive the animation from the SAME posim prompt while the page is open:
#
#          scene start          begin evolving
#          scene pause          freeze
#          scene stop           halt
#          scene reverse        play backwards through the snapshot history
#          scene set_time_step 0.001      seconds of simulated time per frame
#          scene refresh        re-read the body list after adding bodies
#          scene close          shut the window and free the port
#
#      In the page itself: drag with the mouse to orbit, scroll to zoom,
#      shift-drag or the arrow keys to pan, and the on-screen buttons mirror
#      the commands above.
#
#   6. HELP lists every scene command with its arguments.
#
# TROUBLESHOOTING, precisely:
#   * 'no scene window - run SCENE CREATE first' means step 3 was skipped.
#   * A blank page means no bodies existed at 'scene create' time: add them,
#     then issue 'scene refresh'.
#   * If the port is busy, 'scene close' then 'scene create <other port>'.
#   * The window is local only (127.0.0.1). It is not reachable from another
#     machine, by design.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ANIMATION: PARTLY POSSIBLE, WITH THE EXACT LIMIT STATED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The MAIN RESULT of this stage cannot be animated, for a precise reason:
#
#   T(E) is a solution of the TIME-INDEPENDENT Schrodinger equation. It has
#   no time coordinate. There is no state that evolves, so there is nothing
#   to play as a film. Asking for an animation of T(E) is asking for a
#   movie of a stationary quantity.
#
# What you CAN animate is the thing this stage replaced - the wavepacket
# whose average obscured the peaks - and doing so is instructive:
#
#     qm grid -12 12 4800
#     qm potential dbl
#     qm packet -8 1.5 1.0
#     qm method cayley
#     qm animate "double_barrier_packet.html" 12 frames 240
#
# Then open the written file in any browser. On Linux:  xdg-open <file>
# on macOS:  open <file>  - or use the browser's File > Open.
#
# WHAT TO LOOK FOR: the packet hits the barrier, part reflects, a little
# transmits, and the film gives you ONE number for that packet - the
# average of T(E) over its spread. Run 'qm scan' and you get the whole
# curve including both peaks. The contrast between the film and the scan
# IS the stage.
#
# NOTE ON THE 3-D SCENE WINDOW: 'scene create' draws rigid bodies. A
# wavefunction is not one of them, so the window would show an empty grid
# here. The HTML animation above is the correct viewer.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RECREATING THE STAGE SCENARIO
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A double barrier: two walls of height 3, each 0.5 wide, with a well of
# width 3 between them. This language has comparison operators, so the
# piecewise shape is an ordinary user function.
def dbl(x) { 3 * ((abs(x) > 1.5) * (abs(x) < 2)) }

# A domain wide enough that the potential has reached its asymptote at both
# ends, sampled finely enough to resolve the barriers.
qm grid -12 12 4800
qm potential dbl

# THE RESULT THE WAVEPACKET COULD NOT SEE. Scan T(E) at 1200 energies and
# report the interior maxima. Expect two resonances at essentially unit
# transmission - about E = 0.316 and E = 1.288.
qm scan 0.05 2.9 1200

# Between the peaks the same barrier is nearly opaque. Compare:
qm transmission 0.5

# Flux balance, T + R = 1, comes free with the method and holds at every
# energy where the potential is real. It is printed on each line above.
qm transmission 0.316
qm transmission 1.288

# Sharpen the scan around the lower peak to see how narrow it is. This is
# the width that no affordable wavepacket could resolve.
qm scan 0.30 0.33 400

# ===========================================================================
# END OF STAGE #2B NOTEBOOK
# ===========================================================================
